  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-argument essay (with a Terminator-framing metaphor running throughout) aimed at engineering and security teams skeptical that \"agentic AI threats\" are hype. The core thesis: no single AI breakthrough is coming; instead, two ordinary compounding curves multiply — agent *endurance* (METR's task-length doubling every ~4–7 months) and orchestration *breadth* (one orchestrator coordinating dozens of sub-agents, exemplified by Anthropic's GTG-1002 disclosure where Claude Code ran espionage across ~30 targets). The threat emerges in the product of these curves, which scales faster than linear human security review. A key economic point is \"LLMjacking\" (Sysdig): attackers using stolen cloud credentials means capability gates fail because the victim, not the attacker, pays the meter — severing the industry's assumption that users pay for their own usage. Finally, swarm economics make per-attempt failure rates irrelevant since attempts are nearly free (`1 − (1−p)^n` math shows even 0.1% per-attempt success yields ~63% over 1000 tries). Recurring framing: the Terminator's \"absolutely will not stop\" reinterpreted as \"will not stop billing\"; the robot as \"an API call\" to the real villain (the distributed system).",
